# Bench versus rumble

Topic 12, talk 2

After this talk you can:

- compare a model with the thing it models
- say what a bench can and cannot reproduce
- turn "it did not reproduce" into a change to the bench

---

## What the rumble reports

Each RoboRumble pairing is one battle. A robot's rating is its average percentage score (APS) over all pairings, with survival and win rate beside it.

3.4 sits 20th of 1,216 at 85.90 APS ± 0.20, from 1,215 pairings and 1,628 battles.

One pairing is noisy: about ±7.8 APS. So the live page is a lot of noisy rows, and a bench share is a fair stand-in for a pairing only if you check.

Source: README "Standing"; `data/learnings.md` L-02.

---

## L-02: the bench predicted the rumble

On the 20 top-50 opponents that were both benched and fought live:

- live APS tracks bench share, correlation 0.84
- for the 10 fought before the collapse, live minus bench averages -0.1 points
- for the 10 fought after it, -5.9

Later, on the top 19 above 3.4: r = 0.96 over 18 of them, live minus bench +1.0 (SD 4.4) (L-30).

A bench share is a fair stand-in for a healthy live pairing.

---

## Then the live rating collapsed (L-01)

3.0 on 2026-09-28:

| | Pairings | APS | Survival |
|---|---|---|---|
| before 08:30 UTC | 268 | 85.5 | 92.6% |
| after | 239 | 77.2 | 71.2% |

Same opponent strength. Every APS band lost 6 to 10 points at once.

The bench had 3.0 healthy. The model said fine and the world said not fine.

---

## It did not reproduce (L-03, L-21)

The worst live rows were nano and mini bots that Hadur lost 30 to 40% of rounds to. On the bench they were 35 of 35.

The bench was then given a way to vary the client: a forced CPU constant down to 0.3 ms (up to 112 skipped turns a battle), a shared never-wiped data directory, four background-load threads, a data directory built like a long-lived client's.

Survival stayed 97 to 100% in every condition, against a live drop to 71 to 77%.

Each of these ruled something out. That is progress, even with no fix.

---

## What the bench still did differently (L-24)

Every bench pass forked a fresh JVM per battle and met at most 60 distinct opponents.

A rumble client runs a whole session in one process under `-Xmx512M`, against hundreds of opponents.

That difference had never been benched. And it matched the live signature: healthy at first, degrading over an hour or a few hundred battles.

So the bench changed: a session mode (BENCH-6, BENCH-7), 300 battles in one engine process with a control robot.

---

## The session bench finds something (L-26)

3.2 jar, 300 battles in one process, control robot sample.Tracker:

- skipped turns per battle climbed from 10 to 90
- the control stayed at 0
- survival, heap, live classes and turn p95 stayed flat

Every added skip sat one tick after a round's end: the profile save, whose cost grew with the files in the data directory. Wiping the directory between battles held skips flat.

Source: `docs/bench/r7-session.md`. Topic 13 is the full story.

---

## And it was honest about the limit

The session reproduced the skip growth. It did not reproduce the live survival collapse (99.4 to 100% in every block against a live fall from 91.8% to 76.1%).

`docs/skipped-turns-plan.md` says so plainly, narrows the open question to three silent freezes in 10,500 rounds (L-28), and names the next experiment.

When the bench cannot show the effect, write down that it cannot, and say what would.

---

## 3.4 did not slide

3.4's first full pass: 1,215 pairings in 7 hours. Mean APS dipped to 82 to 84 in hours 1 and 2 and then held 86.2 to 86.7 for four hours (L-29). Its last 400 pairings average 86.3 APS against 84.7 for its first 400 (README).

The fix (R8: list the data directory once per battle, keep an index) was benched before it shipped (`docs/bench/r8-memory-scale.md`): 3.3 took 188 to 224 skipped turns per battle on a 700-profile directory at a 1 ms client; 3.4 took 10 to 26.

---

## Lessons

1. A model earns trust by being checked against reality. Do it with numbers (r, mean difference).
2. When it fails, ask what the model leaves out, not what is wrong with reality.
3. Change the bench so it can fail the way the world did.
4. Keep the open items open in writing. L-13 (memory has not paid off against Shadow) and L-28 are still marked open.

Next: the note walks through the bench classes, then the lab builds a small one.

